Backups and restores
06-administrator-commands
The top box names the saved backup directory, says how many archives it holds, and says whether any other session is signed on (a phantom, or one that came in over ssh or through the API). A backup and a restore both run only when every other session has logged out, and LOGIN refuses new ones while they run, so the page lists the other sessions and refuses to show its boxes while they are there. The list is the archives of the directory, newest first: when each was made, how large it is, how many accounts it holds and for which product. The box at the foot shows the manifest of the archive under the cursor, read without unpacking anything.
B backs every account up, which in SD Core Solo is the one account, sduser; A asks for the names; R restores the archive under the cursor; D saves the directory backups go to. Each shows its command (backup.account, restore.account, set.backup.directory) and what it does and runs it only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. A restore replaces the account with the one of the same name in the archive, so its box lists what the archive holds and wants the word RESTORE typed. The account you are using cannot be replaced while SD is running: the verb checks and prepares the archive and says so, and the restore happens the next time SD starts.
SD's own system files and settings are never backed up, and no password or credential is in an archive. SD Core Solo has no root helper: the verbs do the packing and the placing themselves, as the user who runs SD, and where something cannot be done the verb says why and the page shows its words. It is an administrator's page: type ADMIN at the prompt first. F5 reads the directory, the archives and the sessions again.
